<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Iron Gates</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Đerdap, Porțile de Fier</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Iron Gates are the gorge of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> where it cuts through the Carpathian and Balkan mountains, today forming part of the border between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>. For centuries a dangerous stretch for navigation and a strategic crossing point, the area was guarded by Roman and medieval fortresses. The Hungarian Banate of Severin was created around 1230 as a frontier march near the gorge, facing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgarians">Bulgarians</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumans">Cumans</a> and later the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/iron-gates.jpg" alt="Iron Gates" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Cornelius Bechtler · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Danube_near_Iron_Gate_2006.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>In the broad sense the Iron Gates are a route of 134 kilometres on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>, where the river separates the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carpathian-mountains">Carpathian Mountains</a> from the north-western foothills of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkan-mountains">Balkan Mountains</a>. In the narrow sense the name means only the last barrier, just beyond the Romanian city of Orșova. The first narrowing is the Golubac gorge, 14.5 kilometres long, with a medieval fort on the Serbian bank. Next come Gospodjin Vir, with cliffs rising to 500 metres, and the Great and Small Kazan gorges. The Great Kazan is the narrowest, where the river is 150 metres wide and up to 53 metres deep.</p><p>The rapids and rocks in the riverbed made the passage infamous for shipping. Under Ottoman rule local pilots called kalauz guided ships through; under Prince Miloš Obrenović Serbs took over, with a Turkish title, dumendžibaša. The name Đerdap comes from Turkish girdap, meaning whirlpool, and the name Iron Gates translates the Turkish Demir-kapija.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The area has been inhabited for a very long time. The Iron Gates Mesolithic culture, dated about 13,000 to 5,000 years ago, is named after the gorge, and Lepenski Vir, unearthed in the 1960s, is often regarded as the most important Mesolithic site in south-east <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> was part of the Roman frontier here, and the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trajan">Trajan</a> had a bridge built by Apollodorus of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> east of the Great Kazan between 103 and 105, before his final conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dacia">Dacia</a>.</p><p>The islet of Ada Kaleh, a Turkish exclave with about 400 to 500 people, changed hands between Austrians and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> several times from 1669; the Treaty of Lausanne gave it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a> in 1923. In the nineteenth century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istvan-szechenyi">István Széchenyi</a> drafted a plan in 1831 to make the passage navigable, and Hungarian engineers worked from 1889 on channels through the rocks. The Sip Canal was inaugurated on 17 September 1896 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franz-joseph-i">Franz Joseph</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carol-i-of-romania">Carol I of Romania</a> and Alexander Obrenovich of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>A joint Romanian-Yugoslav project began in 1964. The Iron Gate I Dam opened in 1972 and Iron Gate II in 1984, each with a hydroelectric station and navigation locks. The river rose 35 metres near the dam, submerging old Orșova, Ada Kaleh and at least five other villages with 17,000 people, and the dams interrupted the spawning routes of several sturgeon species. The Đerdap National Park in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> dates from 1974 and the Porțile de Fier National Park in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a> from 2001.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Iron Gates” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>King Andrew II's son Béla set up the banate around 1228–1233, in lands taken from Bulgaria and the Cumans east of the <b>Iron Gates</b>, around the fortress of Severin (Drobeta-Turnu Severin) built on Roman ruins.</blockquote><a href="/state/banate-of-severin">Banate of Severin</a></li></ul></section>
